00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:59.927
India: Hello, I am India Anderson
with Avalara, and today I'm going to

00:02:59.927 --> 00:03:06.933
talk with you about my experience from
plugin to product experience and the

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:13.378
accessibility lessons I learned from
improving WooCommerce extension onboarding

00:03:15.618 --> 00:03:19.401
I'll start with telling
you a little bit about me.

00:03:20.685 --> 00:03:25.832
I am a director of product
management at Avalara.

00:03:26.997 --> 00:03:34.577
I have over 10 years of experience
working with WooCommerce and the

00:03:34.579 --> 00:03:42.831
Woo ecosystem, and I personally,
uh, am dyslexic, and I find that my

00:03:42.831 --> 00:03:45.490
dyslexia is actually a superpower.

00:03:46.046 --> 00:03:50.997
I find bugs that no one else finds
because I use accessibility tools.

00:03:51.935 --> 00:03:58.315
Um, I notice things that others skip
over, and so I've been able to amp that

00:03:58.353 --> 00:04:01.355
up within my role as a product manager.

00:04:04.554 --> 00:04:09.590
I wanna start discussing the
accessibility as a checklist problem.

00:04:10.923 --> 00:04:17.324
If you treat accessibility as a
checklist, it means that most of

00:04:17.341 --> 00:04:20.017
the journey never gets checked.

00:04:20.908 --> 00:04:28.249
So a typical checklist is
checking for color contrast.

00:04:29.158 --> 00:04:31.553
Is there alternate text?

00:04:32.258 --> 00:04:33.315
Are there labels?

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.229
What is keyboard navigation like?

00:04:37.456 --> 00:04:42.728
They matter, but they cover
the UI, not the experience.

00:04:44.386 --> 00:04:49.045
A merchant can navigate your
extension with a screen reader

00:04:49.562 --> 00:04:52.414
and still be completely lost.

00:04:54.223 --> 00:05:01.031
There is confusion in onboarding,
uh, silent errors, your documentation

00:05:01.039 --> 00:05:05.973
can be fragmented, and you could
have a dead-end support flow.

00:05:06.592 --> 00:05:09.690
Those are all accessibility barriers too.

00:05:11.162 --> 00:05:16.408
This talk is about expanding the
definition of what accessibility means

00:05:16.776 --> 00:05:19.375
through your reviewing of your extension.

00:05:23.107 --> 00:05:28.891
I'd like to reframe accessibility
as the full product journey.

00:05:30.184 --> 00:05:37.403
Every step a merchant takes can
either reduce or create barriers.

00:05:39.211 --> 00:05:42.937
They go from discovery through to support.

00:05:44.225 --> 00:05:51.916
And today's case study is our WooCommerce
extension, Avalara for WooCommerce.

00:05:53.117 --> 00:05:59.087
My thesis is that accessibility is a
product journey, it's not a feature.

00:06:01.602 --> 00:06:09.155
The full WooCommerce extension journey
goes from discovery to support.

00:06:10.068 --> 00:06:17.355
Today, we will discuss discovery,
installation, account setup,

00:06:18.265 --> 00:06:24.212
configuration, checkout validation,
and support and recovery.

00:06:25.704 --> 00:06:31.811
Each of those stage is part of the
product experience, and each stage

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:35.377
can either reduce or create barriers.

00:06:37.667 --> 00:06:40.403
We'll be going on this journey with Maya.

00:06:41.072 --> 00:06:44.063
Maya is a WooCommerce merchant.

00:06:45.402 --> 00:06:51.888
We're going on the journey with
her as she discovers, installs, and

00:06:51.889 --> 00:06:55.049
configures Avalara for WooCommerce.

00:06:57.631 --> 00:07:02.260
The first step Maya takes
is marketplace discovery.

00:07:03.775 --> 00:07:07.433
What does a merchant see first?

00:07:10.051 --> 00:07:10.832
Within

00:07:13.158 --> 00:07:19.572
Avalara for WooCommerce, we
have a naming inconsistency.

00:07:20.667 --> 00:07:23.842
We have set up complexity
that's not clear.

00:07:24.949 --> 00:07:27.510
There is a jargon-heavy description.

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:36.679
There was no alternate text on the
screenshot, and Maya is confused.

00:07:36.939 --> 00:07:42.988
She's fairly certain that Avalara for
WooCommerce is the product that she needs,

00:07:43.998 --> 00:07:50.406
but she's not entirely certain what it
takes to set it up, and she's sure that

00:07:50.408 --> 00:07:52.270
it has something to do with sales tax.

00:07:52.582 --> 00:07:57.883
So again, it's probably the right
app, but there's a lot of information

00:07:57.885 --> 00:08:01.714
here, and some of it conflicts
with what's listed in other areas.

00:08:05.023 --> 00:08:11.200
The accessibility lesson here is
that consistent naming between the

00:08:11.205 --> 00:08:18.635
UI, documentation, and marketplace
listing is a usability requirement.

00:08:19.275 --> 00:08:20.963
It's not a style preference.

00:08:22.498 --> 00:08:28.445
What we changed was that we
standardized naming across all surfaces.

00:08:29.557 --> 00:08:35.529
For reference, some areas called
it AvaTax, some areas called it

00:08:35.578 --> 00:08:39.850
Avalara, though we did consistently
use the name WooCommerce.

00:08:41.472 --> 00:08:48.663
We also added FAQs, and we improved
the visual hierarchy on our listing.

00:08:51.594 --> 00:08:54.550
Next, Maya's going into installation.

00:08:55.802 --> 00:09:01.896
This is the first moment that a
merchant is touching your actual UI.

00:09:03.662 --> 00:09:09.430
When we started with our WooCommerce
app extension, we had a very

00:09:09.431 --> 00:09:15.057
short message that said that the
plugin had been activated, but we

00:09:15.057 --> 00:09:19.630
essentially dropped the user off at
that stage of the implementation.

00:09:20.898 --> 00:09:23.972
Whereas now, we have a clear next step.

00:09:24.385 --> 00:09:25.658
They need to connect.

00:09:26.118 --> 00:09:33.402
We're giving them a clear call to
action, and that, within accessibility

00:09:33.404 --> 00:09:38.730
terms, means they have clear feedback,
a defined next step, and there is not

00:09:38.772 --> 00:09:42.143
a dead end where there was one before.

00:09:45.758 --> 00:09:51.387
The accessibility lesson here is
that the path to your extension

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:55.415
is part of your product, and
you need to audit it like one.

00:09:56.213 --> 00:10:02.872
So reduce steps, surface what's free
versus paid clearly, test your checkout

00:10:02.876 --> 00:10:07.554
flow, and make sure that you're working
with assistive technologies, and don't

00:10:07.588 --> 00:10:13.033
drop the user off within the middle of a
flow without showing them the next step.

00:10:19.459 --> 00:10:24.381
Now we're with Maya in our
third step of account setup.

00:10:25.146 --> 00:10:32.155
This can be one of the most frustrating
part for our m- merchants because they

00:10:32.156 --> 00:10:36.223
have to connect to a third-party account.

00:10:36.827 --> 00:10:41.909
They've installed Avalara software, but
to connect to the Avalara service, they

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:46.795
have to use their Avalara credentials,
which haven't been required so far.

00:10:47.949 --> 00:10:51.885
They have to configure
business specific settings.

00:10:52.641 --> 00:10:56.917
These settings may be things that
they have not considered so far

00:10:56.920 --> 00:10:58.636
within their WooCommerce journey.

00:10:59.665 --> 00:11:07.058
And they also, ideally, because our app
provides tax calculation related services,

00:11:08.021 --> 00:11:13.622
they would be mapping their products
to tax categories to further tailor how

00:11:13.651 --> 00:11:16.886
our services work within WooCommerce.

00:11:19.403 --> 00:11:25.894
Within our previous onboarding
experience, we saw that there were too

00:11:25.911 --> 00:11:29.289
many decisions within a single screen.

00:11:30.260 --> 00:11:36.117
The fields were not clearly labeled,
there was no in-context help text,

00:11:36.609 --> 00:11:41.824
and again, we had inconsistent
naming between the UI and docs.

00:11:42.612 --> 00:11:48.314
Imagine if you're Maya, and you're
trying to figure out how to make

00:11:48.353 --> 00:11:54.603
a product be taxed as socks, but
in all of the documentation you're

00:11:54.605 --> 00:12:00.078
referencing, it doesn't call them
products, it calls them item codes,

00:12:00.410 --> 00:12:02.833
so nothing come up, up in your search.

00:12:04.031 --> 00:12:07.439
When does that flexibility
become a burden?

00:12:11.966 --> 00:12:18.194
We're helping to reduce the cognitive
load for these users, because cognitive

00:12:18.194 --> 00:12:20.450
load is an accessibility issue.

00:12:21.709 --> 00:12:29.908
In our WooCommerce configuration UI,
we previously had tax class mapping,

00:12:30.236 --> 00:12:36.437
business types, product category mapping,
many, many items that had to be filled at

00:12:36.438 --> 00:12:39.095
once before you could save the changes.

00:12:39.905 --> 00:12:46.675
And though they were all related to
tax, they were not necessarily related

00:12:46.676 --> 00:12:48.745
to each other in a typical setup.

00:12:49.316 --> 00:12:54.234
So some of these items may actually
have been managed by multiple people

00:12:54.584 --> 00:12:56.576
at the WooCommerce merchant's company.

00:12:58.474 --> 00:13:03.969
After our update, we enabled this
setup to be categorized into a few

00:13:03.973 --> 00:13:11.391
basic steps, requiring information
that is related to be filled, and

00:13:11.437 --> 00:13:16.546
they can continue on once that related
information has been completed.

00:13:17.448 --> 00:13:23.711
We also, if the user is connecting
to an Avalara account which they have

00:13:23.711 --> 00:13:29.156
set up previously, are reading that
information from Avalara, what they

00:13:29.159 --> 00:13:34.652
have set in Avalara, so we're not
asking them to actually enter it twice.

00:13:36.040 --> 00:13:38.043
There's a progressive disclosure.

00:13:38.162 --> 00:13:39.690
The settings are grouped together.

00:13:40.215 --> 00:13:42.610
Our labels are written in plain language.

00:13:42.807 --> 00:13:46.872
There's in-context help, and
the defaults are meaningful.

00:13:50.589 --> 00:13:57.930
The accessibility lesson here is that
the cognitive load is a real measurable

00:13:57.935 --> 00:14:03.818
barrier, and the decision to simplify
is a product accessibility decision.

00:14:05.630 --> 00:14:10.964
What changed was simplified visible
settings, progressive disclosure,

00:14:11.193 --> 00:14:13.644
and plain language labels throughout

00:14:16.591 --> 00:14:22.064
Now we're moving to stage five
within our checkout validation.

00:14:23.217 --> 00:14:27.964
This stage can be incredibly
complex for a merchant.

00:14:29.706 --> 00:14:34.521
When they are testing the business
profile settings that they have

00:14:34.521 --> 00:14:40.319
made within Avalara and WooCommerce
and they are creating test orders,

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:46.426
they may not know that they are
actually in a test mode or not.

00:14:47.194 --> 00:14:53.811
They may not recognize where different
data is sourced, whether the result

00:14:53.840 --> 00:15:00.097
they see on screen is due to a setting
in WooCommerce or a setting within

00:15:00.099 --> 00:15:06.772
Avalara, and they may not understand
the tax profile that they've created

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:09.531
and the results that would be expected.

00:15:10.010 --> 00:15:17.564
For example, here we have an
order that has no tax included.

00:15:18.953 --> 00:15:21.740
Do we know why there is no tax?

00:15:22.437 --> 00:15:27.797
Did that order successfully
process and get sent to Avalara?

00:15:28.462 --> 00:15:34.214
Is that the reason, or did they
not set Avalara to calculate

00:15:34.217 --> 00:15:35.809
tax within that region?

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:38.366
You can't tell from this screen.

00:15:40.093 --> 00:15:43.698
So again, no clear test mode indicator.

00:15:44.128 --> 00:15:49.863
There's no clear plain language
success confirmation, and errors

00:15:49.938 --> 00:15:55.379
could often be cryptic, and there's
not a next step of what do you do.

00:15:55.990 --> 00:16:00.882
So Maya's enabled plugin,
she's placed a test order, and

00:16:00.882 --> 00:16:02.640
it has zero dollars of tax.

00:16:04.004 --> 00:16:07.794
Is there anything that helps her
understand if that's correct?

00:16:11.261 --> 00:16:16.184
If that is an error state, every
error state is a design opportunity,

00:16:16.929 --> 00:16:21.078
or it could be a support ticket
that you receive a thousand times.

00:16:22.189 --> 00:16:23.929
So what changed?

00:16:24.501 --> 00:16:30.578
We detect missing tax setup states,
and we surface plain language prompts

00:16:31.242 --> 00:16:33.689
before a connection attempt fails

00:16:36.352 --> 00:16:38.755
Now, let's move to stage six.

00:16:39.205 --> 00:16:45.813
So Maya has discovered, installed,
configured, and tested the

00:16:45.813 --> 00:16:51.887
installation, but now she's looking
at support and documentation.

00:16:53.247 --> 00:16:59.686
A typical frustrated merchant path would
be to go to documentation, potentially

00:16:59.689 --> 00:17:03.132
try live chat, go back to documentation.

00:17:03.598 --> 00:17:11.382
If that doesn't work, file a support
ticket, go back to live chat, maybe

00:17:11.382 --> 00:17:13.381
start over all the way at the beginning.

00:17:14.264 --> 00:17:19.950
If we think back to my previous
explanation, trying to set up product

00:17:19.963 --> 00:17:29.151
taxability, if that product taxability was
labeled product in the UI, but item within

00:17:29.151 --> 00:17:31.997
our documentation, how would Maya find it?

00:17:32.885 --> 00:17:38.840
When Maya sees that zero dollars in
tax and she goes to documentation,

00:17:38.898 --> 00:17:41.981
she may see twenty different
reasons why there's no tax.

00:17:42.570 --> 00:17:47.866
So it's best for us to help her within
the UI to show that, yes, it was a

00:17:47.870 --> 00:17:54.169
successful transaction, and no, there
is no tax because Maya has not set

00:17:54.234 --> 00:17:58.577
tax to be collected within the region
where the transaction was detected.

00:18:03.168 --> 00:18:05.912
Maya at this point is very frustrated.

00:18:06.652 --> 00:18:12.231
Our fragmented support is a barrier,
and it compounds every other barrier.

00:18:13.876 --> 00:18:20.124
Our documentation on woocommerce.com
and Avalara's knowledge base,

00:18:20.411 --> 00:18:21.635
it said different things.

00:18:22.793 --> 00:18:26.937
Merchants got different answers
depending on which they found first.

00:18:28.049 --> 00:18:34.297
Maya was seeing an outdated documentation
on WooCommerce that didn't match

00:18:34.344 --> 00:18:39.443
the newest feature that she had
within her app installation because

00:18:39.504 --> 00:18:44.625
our documentation upload was a day
behind the knowledge center upload.

00:18:46.345 --> 00:18:51.464
She had a support path that was
sent to new users, but Maya did

00:18:51.465 --> 00:18:57.108
not know what login to use when
she got to the Avalara support.

00:18:58.002 --> 00:18:59.408
She was able to use chat.

00:18:59.691 --> 00:19:02.988
When she tried to upload a case,
it asked her for login information

00:19:03.003 --> 00:19:04.510
that she was uncertain of.

00:19:06.079 --> 00:19:14.408
And if a team that's trying to provision
an install account reaches a bot, and

00:19:14.408 --> 00:19:19.198
then there's an email chain, and then
a handoff with no clear resolution,

00:19:19.547 --> 00:19:21.530
that is incredibly frustrating.

00:19:24.874 --> 00:19:30.815
The accessibility lesson here is
not only compounding all of the

00:19:30.818 --> 00:19:36.533
previous steps, again, using the same
terminology in all places, ensuring

00:19:36.546 --> 00:19:38.783
that help is embedded within the UI.

00:19:39.474 --> 00:19:46.401
Repeating issues, making your
re- users repeat themselves and

00:19:46.401 --> 00:19:50.937
re-explain their problem is a
cognitive accessibility failure.

00:19:51.863 --> 00:19:52.778
So what did we do?

00:19:53.540 --> 00:20:00.037
We consolidated documentation, we
streamlined our support routing, and we

00:20:00.039 --> 00:20:03.517
reduced login barriers for new users.

00:20:07.685 --> 00:20:12.322
How does your feedback, how does your
user feedback become requirements?

00:20:13.173 --> 00:20:14.682
Maya has reached out to us.

00:20:14.736 --> 00:20:19.331
She's told us what's wrong,
and so we're listening to Maya.

00:20:19.953 --> 00:20:24.322
You can look at your user reviews,
you can read your support tickets,

00:20:24.889 --> 00:20:29.997
and you can also check compatibility
reports as accessibility research.

00:20:31.207 --> 00:20:36.929
A low star rating on your user
reviews may not be a brand problem.

00:20:37.447 --> 00:20:45.774
It could merely be a signal that your
UI, your app itself is not accessible.

00:20:46.182 --> 00:20:46.978
It's a signal map.

00:20:48.553 --> 00:20:56.015
There can be patterns across negative
reviews that indicate update instability,

00:20:56.624 --> 00:21:05.280
uh, unexpected fees, repeated errors,
support delays, or common setup confusion.

00:21:06.132 --> 00:21:11.004
And you can trace those patterns back
to specific stages within the journey.

00:21:11.813 --> 00:21:16.982
Again, how many times did a user call
out that it took them five reviews of

00:21:16.982 --> 00:21:23.140
documentation before they figured out
that product was item in the document,

00:21:23.368 --> 00:21:29.043
and that's what they needed to see in
order to figure out how to map a tax code

00:21:31.101 --> 00:21:37.010
The process to utilize feedback and
turn it into requirements is to ensure

00:21:37.010 --> 00:21:39.418
you're collecting feedback, first of all.

00:21:39.945 --> 00:21:45.207
So make sure that you are memorializing
those support inquiries and that you are

00:21:45.209 --> 00:21:50.375
taking time to review the app reviews and
feedback that you get from your users.

00:21:52.488 --> 00:21:55.559
Categorize that feedback that you receive.

00:21:56.360 --> 00:21:58.281
Apply keyword search.

00:21:58.928 --> 00:22:01.919
Look for frequent repeating phrases.

00:22:02.562 --> 00:22:09.365
And if you have it instrumented within
your app, look to see at what stage

00:22:09.426 --> 00:22:11.814
those users are typically contacting you.

00:22:12.464 --> 00:22:22.241
Can you tell if those users that all
mention password were actually connected

00:22:22.714 --> 00:22:28.921
successfully, and maybe that is related
to support rather than connecting the app?

00:22:29.853 --> 00:22:36.368
Or can you tell that users who are in
the stage of the journey where they have

00:22:36.391 --> 00:22:42.240
connected the app and they're running test
transactions are questioning tax, or is

00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:44.718
that happening before they've connected?

00:22:45.527 --> 00:22:52.678
Associate and categorize, again,
looking for keyword search, like

00:22:52.985 --> 00:22:57.823
statements, and whether users are in
a particular part of their journey.

00:22:59.110 --> 00:23:03.328
Tracing those to the journey stage
gives the issues more meaning.

00:23:03.890 --> 00:23:12.218
Again, a user who has connected but is
not calculating would be very different

00:23:12.473 --> 00:23:17.189
than a user who has not successfully
connected and is not calculating.

00:23:18.495 --> 00:23:22.574
And then prioritize by
frequency and impact.

00:23:24.314 --> 00:23:31.363
If your user can access the service,
engage with the service, but they're

00:23:31.415 --> 00:23:38.177
tailoring it to their own needs, that
would be a very different experience

00:23:38.435 --> 00:23:41.110
than if they cannot even connect.

00:23:41.897 --> 00:23:47.522
Both of them are high priority, but
one user has gotten much further

00:23:47.522 --> 00:23:48.830
in the journey than the other.

00:23:55.104 --> 00:24:00.723
The accessibility lesson is that if
users can't use your product without

00:24:00.775 --> 00:24:05.746
struggling, that's an accessibility
issue regardless of whether it

00:24:05.746 --> 00:24:08.418
shows up in a screen reader audit.

00:24:09.397 --> 00:24:14.456
And what changed at Avalara is
that we reviewed specific merchant

00:24:14.458 --> 00:24:19.601
feedback, and that drove us to
make changes within onboarding and

00:24:19.603 --> 00:24:22.352
simplifying that onboarding experience.

00:24:26.390 --> 00:24:32.418
The accessibility audit framework that I
would suggest applying to your extension

00:24:32.915 --> 00:24:38.939
is first and foremost, figure out what
your extension's life cycle stages are.

00:24:39.448 --> 00:24:45.258
Again, the users mentioning having
password issues when they have connected

00:24:45.262 --> 00:24:50.716
versus when they haven't, or seeing that
they are not getting the results they

00:24:50.716 --> 00:24:56.198
expected after they've connected versus
before are very different problems.

00:24:56.601 --> 00:25:02.315
And so you need to understand the life
cycle stages of your extension to be able

00:25:02.315 --> 00:25:08.091
to tie those back to a particular area of
the experience that you need to work on

00:25:10.211 --> 00:25:15.360
It's best to try to walk each
stage as a first-time merchant.

00:25:16.137 --> 00:25:22.291
So try to push what you know about how the
app is supposed to work out of your head.

00:25:23.046 --> 00:25:28.702
Go through the experience
without automatically selecting

00:25:28.702 --> 00:25:30.261
something on the screen.

00:25:31.149 --> 00:25:32.073
Take a pause.

00:25:32.413 --> 00:25:35.019
Look at the whole screen
as though it was new.

00:25:36.072 --> 00:25:44.299
Are there multiple call to actions that
should be shown at the same time, but one

00:25:44.429 --> 00:25:46.428
is clearly higher priority than the other?

00:25:46.992 --> 00:25:52.319
Is there a single call to action
where that call to action is clear?

00:25:55.490 --> 00:25:58.379
Judge your app as though you
were a first-time merchant.

00:25:59.726 --> 00:26:06.110
And as you're going through,
make notes of any info that you

00:26:06.168 --> 00:26:08.408
needed but you weren't given.

00:26:09.243 --> 00:26:16.341
So for example, if the point at which
you enter credentials doesn't have any

00:26:16.342 --> 00:26:22.804
information about how you should have
received those credentials, or if your

00:26:22.805 --> 00:26:28.137
service has multiple credential types,
the kind of credentials that should be

00:26:28.161 --> 00:26:35.185
entered within a particular area, then
that could cause onboarding friction.

00:26:35.627 --> 00:26:42.409
Help your users by helping them to
understand that this was a key that you

00:26:42.409 --> 00:26:48.918
would've gotten in the UI or a login that
was sent to them via email, et cetera.

00:26:51.647 --> 00:26:57.166
And as you're going through that journey,
and you've noted moments you weren't

00:26:57.180 --> 00:27:03.310
given the info that you needed, you
also need to flag every place where you

00:27:03.311 --> 00:27:06.417
could get stuck with no clear next step.

00:27:08.066 --> 00:27:16.235
If your app onboarding is leading users
to connection as the biggest goal, and

00:27:16.385 --> 00:27:22.386
there's no leading into the next step
of how to test the service that they

00:27:22.393 --> 00:27:29.069
have embedded, purchased, if there's
no next step to tell them any type of

00:27:29.069 --> 00:27:35.845
maintenance, or how frequently they
should review settings, et cetera, then

00:27:35.943 --> 00:27:43.091
the user may have successfully installed
but fail at using the app ongoing.

00:27:44.048 --> 00:27:51.629
They also may miss the value proposition
that they expected if you're leading

00:27:51.629 --> 00:27:56.232
them to the onboarding success,
but you're not leading them within

00:27:56.236 --> 00:27:58.684
the successful use of your service.

00:28:00.526 --> 00:28:05.759
And last but not least, in your
accessibility audit framework,

00:28:06.191 --> 00:28:08.400
check your support tickets.

00:28:09.365 --> 00:28:14.379
Make sure that your users have the
opportunity to give you feedback.

00:28:14.864 --> 00:28:19.968
Make sure how they can give you
feedback is clearly shown within your

00:28:19.975 --> 00:28:23.646
UI so that you get that feedback.

00:28:23.955 --> 00:28:26.594
And once you have it, review it.

00:28:27.115 --> 00:28:33.298
Categorize those support issues, again,
keywords and looking for common themes.

00:28:34.066 --> 00:28:37.661
Judge them based on where those users
were within the support journey.

00:28:38.766 --> 00:28:41.925
And those patterns can help you…

00:28:42.552 --> 00:28:48.002
That accessibility audit can help
you to make your product better.

00:28:52.893 --> 00:28:58.056
Here is a practical framework
for evaluating your extension.

00:28:59.134 --> 00:29:03.266
Again, walk as though you don't
know how it already works.

00:29:04.807 --> 00:29:11.750
Go through discovery, installation,
configuration, checkout

00:29:11.752 --> 00:29:15.717
validation, support, and feedback.

00:29:17.042 --> 00:29:19.889
Again, look for naming consistencies.

00:29:20.999 --> 00:29:22.173
Count your steps.

00:29:22.775 --> 00:29:27.130
Try using accessibility tools
while you are going through

00:29:27.661 --> 00:29:29.429
the installation and use.

00:29:30.986 --> 00:29:33.710
Look at what settings are truly required.

00:29:34.096 --> 00:29:36.908
Are you forcing users to make
a decision about something

00:29:36.908 --> 00:29:38.686
that could be left until later?

00:29:39.537 --> 00:29:43.331
If they did skip something that
you have made skippable but it

00:29:43.341 --> 00:29:49.311
makes the service unusable, I
would suggest that you update that.

00:29:50.353 --> 00:29:54.786
Uh, is the language plain, or do
you have jargon within your UI?

00:29:56.405 --> 00:30:04.409
When they're in the validation phase,
try to cause every error that you can.

00:30:05.506 --> 00:30:08.838
See what happens when you
enter wrong credentials.

00:30:09.248 --> 00:30:13.374
See what happens if you try to
connect a sandbox in production.

00:30:14.871 --> 00:30:19.476
Really try to validate things that
your users may surprise you with.

00:30:20.672 --> 00:30:22.666
And try your own support.

00:30:23.143 --> 00:30:24.846
See how you can get help.

00:30:25.526 --> 00:30:28.610
How many steps does it take
to actually get support?

00:30:29.187 --> 00:30:34.104
Were there handoffs, and do you have to
repeat yourself, or is the information

00:30:34.104 --> 00:30:38.837
that they enter first carried through
until the end of the support journey?

00:30:39.832 --> 00:30:41.886
And check feedback.

00:30:42.374 --> 00:30:46.096
What do your one and two
star ratings actually say?

00:30:46.611 --> 00:30:48.968
And try to map them to a stage

00:30:51.918 --> 00:30:58.221
I have three things that I suggest
that you take back to your plugin to

00:30:58.237 --> 00:31:03.819
enable accessibility across the full
extension journey starting this week.

00:31:04.653 --> 00:31:06.660
One, map the journey.

00:31:06.929 --> 00:31:11.157
Again, drawing every step from
your marketplace discovery

00:31:11.503 --> 00:31:12.611
all the way to support.

00:31:13.244 --> 00:31:15.969
You probably have gaps
that you haven't named yet

00:31:18.026 --> 00:31:22.250
Second, audit one stage this sprint.

00:31:22.823 --> 00:31:27.239
Within that journey, pick a
stage that you know the least.

00:31:27.705 --> 00:31:30.610
Pick the part that you
haven't really reviewed.

00:31:31.343 --> 00:31:36.361
Typically, it's support or potentially
validation that your users do.

00:31:36.822 --> 00:31:39.621
Walk through it as though
you were a new user.

00:31:40.645 --> 00:31:44.885
And three, treat feedback
as accessibility data.

00:31:45.456 --> 00:31:51.901
Support tickets, low star reviews,
compatibility reports and exceptions, they

00:31:51.904 --> 00:31:55.147
are telling you where the barriers are.

00:31:56.158 --> 00:32:00.129
Accessible plugins and accessible
plugin experiences, they

00:32:00.151 --> 00:32:02.110
aren't built in one release.

00:32:02.816 --> 00:32:05.879
They're built one honest audit at a time.

00:32:09.443 --> 00:32:12.437
All right, so that is the
end of my presentation.

