ma- a, a time, like, in your
experience that made you

00:43:11.354 --> 00:43:13.539
realize a grouping of questions?

00:43:13.539 --> 00:43:18.391
Like, like, a ti- Mm-hmm … your, your
favorite, "Oh, I need to start asking

00:43:18.391 --> 00:43:22.124
this going forward," moment, your big aha.

00:43:22.124 --> 00:43:23.196
Mm. Can you tell me about that?

00:43:23.200 --> 00:43:24.670
Do you have anything off
the top of your head?

00:43:25.409 --> 00:43:26.455
India: Um, well, yeah.

00:43:26.455 --> 00:43:31.993
So I, I think it's, it's typically
with, um, like combati- compatibility

00:43:31.993 --> 00:43:34.407
or initial installation questions.

00:43:34.847 --> 00:43:40.042
Um, you, you learn how to ask
questions about, like, okay, so

00:43:40.054 --> 00:43:43.901
you're having X error, or it's
dropping you into a blank screen.

00:43:44.248 --> 00:43:48.241
Um, there are some typical questions,
of course, to ask, like, what…

00:43:48.321 --> 00:43:52.591
Uh, again, because this is, uh, web
software, what browser are you using?

00:43:52.591 --> 00:43:54.605
What, what version is that browser on?

00:43:55.028 --> 00:43:59.048
Uh, is this the first, uh,
third-party app you've installed in

00:43:59.049 --> 00:44:02.312
your instance, or are there other
third-party apps that are installed?

00:44:02.746 --> 00:44:04.481
Um, have, have…

00:44:04.516 --> 00:44:06.418
Is this a relatively new setup?

00:44:06.694 --> 00:44:11.015
Um, do you have established
customized workflows in behavior

00:44:11.016 --> 00:44:12.694
in this area, in this area?

00:44:13.072 --> 00:44:16.504
Um, and so those are pretty basic
questions, especially when we're

00:44:16.505 --> 00:44:20.846
talking about extensions, to ask to
understand because, you know, i- there

00:44:20.849 --> 00:44:24.754
could be a compatibility issue where
a particular element doesn't load

00:44:24.764 --> 00:44:26.407
because they're on a really old version.

00:44:26.798 --> 00:44:31.191
Um, uh, I, I can tell you, um,
you, you will hear some surprising

00:44:31.202 --> 00:44:35.614
things, um, with, you know, not m-
not maintaining or updating browser.

00:44:35.924 --> 00:44:40.390
Um, I even spoke to a customer once who,
um, they had… Uh, this is a while ago,

00:44:41.338 --> 00:44:44.805
but they had IE6, Internet Explorer 6.

00:44:44.855 --> 00:44:49.046
And because they had that, our buttons
couldn't show because they required

00:44:49.047 --> 00:44:50.427
a security layer that was not-

00:44:50.625 --> 00:44:50.647
Kadian: Mm-hmm.

00:44:50.647 --> 00:44:50.898
India: Right?

00:44:50.900 --> 00:44:55.179
So, so when, when you're thinking about
those kind of things, because this is,

00:44:55.219 --> 00:44:59.326
uh, browser, browser use, web-based
use, um, make sure to ask about browser,

00:44:59.329 --> 00:45:00.663
make sure to ask about compatibility.

00:45:00.718 --> 00:45:02.623
Ask about the environment that they're in.

00:45:02.956 --> 00:45:06.488
Isolate how long they've been using the
software, what variables there are there.

00:45:06.803 --> 00:45:10.883
Um, and then also the kind of simple,
straightforward things about, uh,

00:45:11.172 --> 00:45:14.247
are… Is it happening for just their
user or other users of the company?

00:45:14.287 --> 00:45:15.363
What's their role?

00:45:15.389 --> 00:45:17.734
Um, like, as in, are they the only admin?

00:45:17.736 --> 00:45:19.281
Are there other admins
who experience that?

00:45:19.546 --> 00:45:20.367
Those kind of things.

00:45:20.665 --> 00:45:23.980
Um, I, I think that's kind of more
within the, the troubleshooting realm.

00:45:24.314 --> 00:45:28.880
Um, but when you have, um, something
like, um, like what I talked about,

00:45:29.255 --> 00:45:35.420
like, um, uh, words for a field that's
in the UI having a different word than

00:45:35.422 --> 00:45:39.240
the word that you use in documentation,
for those kind of things, what you wanna

00:45:39.250 --> 00:45:44.214
ask yourself is, is simply the thing that
you show your user, is that the thing

00:45:44.217 --> 00:45:45.791
that you show your user consistently?

00:45:45.833 --> 00:45:46.427
Um, and-

00:45:46.486 --> 00:45:46.496
Kadian: Mm-hmm

00:45:46.496 --> 00:45:51.366
… India: especially for call to action, um,
calls to action in the UI, make sure the

00:45:51.369 --> 00:45:55.345
words that are used there are actually
used in other places in the documentation.

00:45:55.661 --> 00:45:59.741
Um, if you've got a user who looks
up, "Oh, activate. I don't know

00:45:59.743 --> 00:46:00.888
if I should click this button."

00:46:01.361 --> 00:46:01.484
Kadian: Mm-hmm.

00:46:01.484 --> 00:46:03.886
India: Yeah, they're probably
gonna search activate in your

00:46:03.886 --> 00:46:07.174
documentation even though you may not
think that that needs to be listed.

00:46:07.582 --> 00:46:08.750
That's the call to action.

00:46:08.820 --> 00:46:13.817
It's the word activate, so make sure that
that word, um, it would show up in search.

00:46:13.904 --> 00:46:17.866
Um, and be really careful to not
have things only embedded in images

00:46:17.868 --> 00:46:19.625
that don't have alternate text-

00:46:19.842 --> 00:46:19.866
Kadian: Mm-hmm

00:46:19.866 --> 00:46:21.739
… India: 'cause you wanna make
sure that things are searchable.

00:46:22.620 --> 00:46:24.922
